-- allium: 3
-- vados.allium
--
-- Vados: a static site generator that turns a directory tree of content into a
-- Bulma-styled HTML site.
--
-- Scope:
--   The whole generator, at domain level. One maintainer authors a source tree
--   and an image tree and runs the tool from the command line. The tool has
--   three subcommands: `generate` reads the trees and publishes a site,
--   `check` reads the same trees and reports every problem it can find without
--   writing anything, and `init` scaffolds a fresh project -- gathering a few
--   basics and creating a starting source tree, image tree and Netlify
--   deployment setup -- before there is anything for the other two to read.
--   Six further subcommands author content into a project that already
--   exists, one piece at a time, instead of reading it wholesale the way
--   `generate`/`check` do: `page new` creates a page, `image add` registers an
--   image, `page add-image` attaches an image (freshly registered or already
--   declared) to a page, `social add`/`social update`/`social remove` and
--   `footer set` adjust menu.json's social links and main.json's footer, and
--   `menu add-item` appends an entry to menu.json's main menu.
--
-- Includes:
--   - The command line: the `generate`, `check` and `init` subcommands, and
--     the six content-authoring subcommands (`page new`, `image add`,
--     `page add-image`, `social add`/`update`/`remove`, `footer set`,
--     `menu add-item`)
--   - Pre-flight validation: findings, their severity, and the check outcome
--   - Project scaffolding: what `init` creates, the basics and social handles
--     it gathers with their defaults, its refusal to overwrite anything
--     already there, and its idempotent handling of version control
--   - Authoring content into an existing project: creating a page, registering
--     an image, attaching an image to a page as its hero or as a notification,
--     adding, updating or removing a social link or the footer, and appending
--     an entry to the main menu -- each checked against the same problems
--     `check` itself would find, before anything is written
--   - Site-wide configuration (title, footer, extra CSS/JS, background, navbar)
--   - The Bulma design commitment: semantic colours, aspect-ratio classes and
--     the default Bulma/Material-Design-icon stylesheets are first-class here,
--     because the project is built around Bulma (see README)
--   - Content structure: one page per source directory, page hierarchy,
--     sibling display order
--   - Menus and navigation: main menu with dropdowns, side menu, breadcrumbs,
--     social links with provider auto-detection
--   - Notifications: configured left/right notifications, subtree inheritance,
--     and auto-generated side notifications
--   - Images: aspect-ratio matching, centre cropping, responsive variant set,
--     and the Main/Sub/Side placement distinction
--   - Page assembly and publication
--
-- Excludes (implementation, deliberately not modelled):
--   - The template engine and the individual template files / their markup
--   - Concurrency, shared ownership and the caching data structures
--   - JSON parsing mechanics (main.json, menu.json, page.json and images.json
--     are named only as the surfaces the maintainer authors)
--   - Directory walking and path string manipulation
--   - Image decoding/resizing/encoding libraries, the resampling filter,
--     alpha premultiplication and WebP encoding quality
--   - CSS class name spelling (`is-primary`, `is-16by9`, ...). The Color and
--     ImageRatio enumerations are domain; their class names are not
--   - HTML minification of the produced output
--   - Pixel breakpoints and the `sizes`/`decoding`/`loading` attribute values.
--     The Main/Sub/Side placement distinction is domain; the pixels are not
--   - The interactive prompt sequence `init` uses to gather basics. Only the
--     bundle of basics gathered and their defaults are domain, the same way
--     JSON parsing mechanics are excluded above
--   - The literal contents of every file `init` creates (netlify.toml,
--     package.json, the Sass entry file, main.json, menu.json, page.json, the
--     README) and the exact shell commands a Netlify build runs. Only the
--     artifact categories and what they are wired from are domain
--   - Installing Rust, the `vados` binary or npm packages during a Netlify
--     build
--   - The `git` invocation and the literal lines a .gitignore ends up with.
--     Only the choice to initialize-if-absent and merge-if-present is domain
--   - The literal mechanics of reading, modifying and rewriting main.json,
--     menu.json, page.json or images.json when authoring content into an
--     existing project. Only the bundle of inputs gathered and the resulting
--     outcome are domain -- the same judgement already made for JSON parsing
--     mechanics above
--   - The interactive prompt sequences the six content-authoring subcommands
--     use to gather whatever input the maintainer didn't supply as a flag.
--     Only the bundle of inputs gathered is domain, the same as `init`'s own
--     prompt sequence is excluded above

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The single maintainer who authors the source tree and runs the generator.
-- This is a solo project: there is exactly one operator and no roles.
external entity Maintainer {}

-- Someone reading the published site. Modelled only so the shape of a
-- published page can be stated as a boundary.
external entity Visitor {}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- How a piece of content is referred to from configuration. One of: a path to
-- a Markdown file, a path to an HTML file, or a literal HTML fragment
-- (recognised by ending in a closing tag). A reference matching none of the
-- three is `unrecognised`: it is retained rather than rejected on the spot, so
-- that a check run can report it as a finding alongside everything else.
value ContentReference {
    reference: String
    kind: ContentKind

    -- Markdown is converted to HTML; HTML files and inline HTML are used
    -- as written.
    --
    -- Two failure modes are deliberately treated differently, and the
    -- asymmetry is intentional rather than an oversight:
    --
    --   - A reference matching none of the three recognised forms is an
    --     authoring mistake. The maintainer wrote something that cannot mean
    --     anything, so the run fails loudly rather than quietly publishing a
    --     page with a hole in it. A check run reports it as an error instead
    --     of failing, which is the same judgement expressed without aborting.
    --   - A reference of a recognised form whose file is not there is
    --     ordinary: files get renamed, moved or deleted while a site is being
    --     edited. The content resolves to empty, a warning is reported, and
    --     the rest of the site still publishes.
    html: resolve_content(reference, kind)
}

-- One social handle the maintainer gave `init` for a recognised provider.
-- Turning a handle into the platform's full profile URL is implementation,
-- the same way JSON parsing mechanics are excluded elsewhere in this spec.
value SocialHandle {
    provider: SocialProvider
    handle: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Bulma's semantic colour palette. Used for the navbar and for notifications.
enum Color {
    white | black | light | dark | primary | link | info | success | warning | danger
}

-- The standard aspect ratios an image can be cropped to. Every source image is
-- matched to the closest of these.
enum ImageRatio {
    `1:1` | `5:4` | `4:3` | `3:2` | `5:3` | `16:9` | `2:1` | `3:1` |
    `4:5` | `3:4` | `2:3` | `3:5` | `9:16` | `1:2` | `1:3`
}

-- Where an image sits on a page. Placement determines loading priority and
-- display size: a hero image is fetched eagerly and shown large, notification
-- images are deferred and shown smaller.
enum ImagePlacement { main | sub | side }

enum ContentKind { markdown_file | html_file | inline_html | unrecognised }

-- What the maintainer asked the tool to do. A generate run publishes a site; a
-- check run reads the same trees, reports what is wrong and writes nothing.
enum RunMode { generate | check }

-- Whether a finding fails the check. Fixed policy: see Finding.severity.
enum FindingSeverity { error | warning }

-- What kind of problem a check run found. Every kind names one authoring
-- mistake the maintainer can act on.
enum FindingKind {
    -- Something in the source or image tree could not be read as what it
    -- claims to be. Reported by whatever reads the tree, not derived.
    site_config_unreadable
    menu_unreadable
    page_config_unreadable
    images_manifest_unreadable

    -- Content references.
    unrecognised_content_reference
    missing_content_file

    -- Menus and social links.
    external_menu_link_without_title
    unresolved_menu_link
    unrecognised_social_link_incomplete

    -- Images.
    duplicate_image_reference
    image_without_alternative_text
    unknown_page_image_reference
    unknown_notification_image_reference
    missing_image_source_file

    -- Notifications.
    dead_internal_notification_link
}

enum MenuLinkKind { internal | external }

enum NotificationKind { plain | internal | external }

enum NotificationSide { left | right }

-- Social providers recognised from their URL. Recognised providers get a
-- canonical icon and brand colour; anything else must supply its own.
--
-- `twitter` recognises both the `twitter.com` and `x.com` domains as the same
-- provider; the rebrand changed the URL, not the identity of the account.
enum SocialProvider { github | linked_in | facebook | youtube | twitter | other }

-- What `init` would create in the maintainer's current directory. Named so a
-- conflict can say which artifact is in the way, not just that something is.
enum ScaffoldArtifactKind {
    site_config       -- main.json
    menu_config       -- menu.json
    home_page         -- page.json for the site root
    image_tree        -- the image tree's root directory
    stylesheet_entry  -- the Sass file overriding Bulma's $primary
    package_manifest  -- package.json and its Sass dev-dependency
    netlify_config    -- netlify.toml
    readme            -- README.md
}

-- Why a `page new` request couldn't proceed. Recorded rather than raised, so
-- every problem with the request is found in one pass -- the same judgement
-- ScaffoldArtifactKind's conflict check and CheckInvocation both already
-- make -- instead of the maintainer discovering them one attempt at a time.
enum PageCreationBlockReason {
    path_already_a_page | parent_path_is_not_a_page | image_reference_unresolved
}

-- Why an `image add` (or the image-registering form of `page add-image`)
-- request couldn't proceed.
enum ImageRegistrationBlockReason { source_file_unreadable | reference_key_already_used }

-- Why a `page add-image` request couldn't proceed. Shares its middle two
-- reasons with ImageRegistrationBlockReason (registering a new image is the
-- same problem however it's reached) plus two of its own: the target page
-- must already exist, and an existing reference must resolve.
enum PageImageAttachmentBlockReason {
    target_page_missing | source_file_unreadable | reference_key_already_used |
    image_reference_unresolved | hero_already_present
}

-- Whether an image `page add-image` attaches fills a page's one hero slot or
-- joins its notifications instead. See PageImageAttachmentRun.effective_role.
enum PageImageRole { hero | notification }

-- Which of the three social-link authoring commands a SocialLinkChangeRun
-- performs -- the same technique GenerationRun.mode already uses to share one
-- entity between `generate` and `check`.
enum SocialLinkChangeKind { add | update | remove }

-- Why a `social add`, `social update` or `social remove` request was
-- rejected. Unlike the block reasons above, a social-link change carries at
-- most one: matching an existing link and judging completeness are both
-- cheap enough that there is never more than one thing wrong to report.
enum SocialLinkChangeRejectionReason {
    unrecognised_provider_incomplete | url_already_registered |
    no_matching_social_link | ambiguous_social_link_match | match_index_out_of_range
}

-- Why a `menu add-item` request was rejected. Mirrors
-- ExternalMenuLinkCarriesTitle for the one way an add could break a stated
-- invariant, plus one added-command-specific check against duplicating an
-- entry already there. An internal url naming a path with no page yet is
-- deliberately not a rejection reason -- see MenuLinkAdditionRun.would_resolve.
enum MenuLinkAdditionRejectionReason { external_link_without_title | url_already_in_menu }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One invocation of the tool. A batch process: it is requested once, reads the
-- source and image trees, and ends either with a published site or with a
-- report of what is wrong.
--
-- Both subcommands are the same run up to the point where the structure is
-- known. Discovery is shared wholesale: site configuration, menu links, social
-- links, pages, notifications and images are declared identically whatever the
-- mode. Only after `structure_built` do the two diverge: a generate run
-- assembles and publishes, a check run evaluates the structure it just built
-- and reports.
entity GenerationRun {
    mode: RunMode
    source_location: String
    image_source_location: String

    -- Only a generate run has somewhere to put a site. A check run produces no
    -- output, so it is given no destination at all; see CheckRunHasNoDestination.
    destination: String?

    status: requested | configured | structure_built | published | checked

    transitions status {
        requested -> configured
        configured -> structure_built
        structure_built -> published
        structure_built -> checked
        terminal: published, checked
    }

    site_config: SiteConfig with run = this
    pages: Page with run = this
    main_menu_links: MenuLink with run = this
    social_links: SocialLink with run = this

    -- Every notification declared anywhere beneath the source root, reachable
    -- without walking the page tree.
    notifications: Notification with page.run = this

    -- The images this run declares. This is the set the destination is
    -- brought into line with: variants left at the destination by earlier
    -- runs, for images that are not in this set, are no longer owned.
    images: SiteImage with run = this

    -- Only entries that resolve reach the published site. An internal entry
    -- naming a path with no page is reported and dropped, not fatal.
    published_main_menu_links: main_menu_links where resolves

    -- Everything a check run found. A run collects findings; it never stops at
    -- one. The outcome is a count, not a first failure.
    findings: Finding with run = this
    errors: findings where severity = error
    warnings: findings where severity = warning
    error_count: errors.count
    warning_count: warnings.count

    -- The check passes exactly when nothing error-severity was found. Warnings
    -- are reported and counted but never change this.
    check_passed: error_count = 0
}

-- Site-wide configuration, authored as main.json at the source root.
entity SiteConfig {
    run: GenerationRun
    site_title: String
    footer_content: ContentReference

    extra_css_files: List<String>
    extra_js_files: List<String>
    include_default_css: Boolean?
    include_default_js: Boolean?

    background_class: String?
    navbar_color: Color?

    -- Defaults apply when the maintainer says nothing.
    default_css_included: include_default_css ?? true
    default_js_included: include_default_js ?? true
    background: background_class ?? config.default_background_class
    navbar: navbar_color ?? config.default_navbar_color

    -- The default stylesheets are the Bulma stylesheet and a Material Design
    -- icon font; the default script supplies the burger-menu and side-menu
    -- toggling behaviour. Maintainer-supplied files come first.
    css_links: if default_css_included:
                   concat(extra_css_files, config.default_css_links)
               else: extra_css_files
    js_links: if default_js_included:
                  concat(extra_js_files, config.default_js_links)
              else: extra_js_files
}

-- One page of the site. Every directory under the source root is one page;
-- directory nesting is page nesting. The source root is the page at "/".
entity Page {
    run: GenerationRun
    path: String
    parent: Page?
    title: String
    content: ContentReference
    sub_title: String?
    icon: String?
    summary: String?
    image_reference: String?
    order: Integer?
    status: discovered | published

    transitions status {
        discovered -> published
        terminal: published
    }

    is_root: parent = null
    children: Page with parent = this

    -- Includes this page itself; the pages under the same parent.
    siblings: Page with parent = this.parent

    notifications: Notification with page = this
    own_left_notifications: notifications where side = left
    own_right_notifications: notifications where side = right

    -- The page's hero image, if it names one that was declared and rendered.
    main_image: SiteImage with reference_key = this.image_reference and run = this.run
    main_image_placement: config.hero_image_placement

    -- Number of path segments. The root and top-level pages both count as 1,
    -- "/a/b" as 2, and so on.
    depth: segment_count(path)

    -- Sibling display order: pages with an explicit order come first, in
    -- ascending order; pages without one come last; ties break alphabetically
    -- by title.
    precedes(other):
        if order != null and other.order = null: true
        else if order = null and other.order != null: false
        else if order != null and other.order != null and order != other.order: order < other.order
        else: title < other.title

    ordered_children: in_display_order(children)
    ordered_siblings: in_display_order(siblings)

    -- Notifications configured on a page apply to that page and to everything
    -- beneath it: a descendant with none of its own shows the nearest
    -- ancestor's. Left and right inherit independently.
    effective_left_notifications:
        if own_left_notifications.count > 0: own_left_notifications
        else: parent?.effective_left_notifications ?? {}
    effective_right_notifications:
        if own_right_notifications.count > 0: own_right_notifications
        else: parent?.effective_right_notifications ?? {}

    -- Separately from configured notifications, every page advertises a few
    -- neighbouring pages: its own children when it has any, otherwise its
    -- siblings. Taken from the end of display order, never including itself.
    --
    -- Children-first is deliberate, not a fallback ordering that happens to
    -- exist. A page that has a section beneath it points readers down into
    -- that section, because that is where the site continues. Only a childless
    -- page, having nothing below it to offer, points sideways at its peers.
    side_notification_source: if children.count > 0: ordered_children else: ordered_siblings
    side_notification_pages:
        last_excluding(side_notification_source, this, config.max_side_notifications)
    side_notification_image_placement: config.side_notification_image_placement
    side_notification_color: config.side_notification_color

    -- Breadcrumbs appear once a page is nested far enough to be worth
    -- retracing: the ancestor chain from the root down to, but not including,
    -- this page. The site root is deliberately the first crumb, so the trail
    -- always reaches all the way back to the site index rather than stopping
    -- at whichever ancestor happens to be within the depth threshold.
    shows_breadcrumbs: depth >= config.breadcrumb_min_depth
    breadcrumb_trail: ancestor_chain(this)

    -- The side menu offers in-section navigation on deeply nested pages. It is
    -- rooted at this page when it has children, otherwise at its parent.
    side_menu_root: if children.count > 0: this else: parent
    shows_side_menu:
        depth >= config.side_menu_min_depth
        and (children.count > 0 or depth > config.side_menu_min_depth)

    -- Where the page lands on the published site: the root page becomes the
    -- site index, "/foo/bar" becomes "foo/bar".
    output_location: page_output_location(path)
}

-- A notification the maintainer attached to a page, in the left or right
-- column. Notifications are inherited by the page's whole subtree.
entity Notification {
    page: Page
    side: NotificationSide
    position: Integer
    content: ContentReference
    title: String?
    image_reference: String?
    url: String?
    given_color: Color?

    -- A notification with no link is plain; one linking within the site is
    -- internal; anything else points off-site. Classified once, from the url.
    kind: NotificationKind

    -- An internal notification points at a path within this site. Unlike a main
    -- menu entry, a notification is published whether or not its target exists:
    -- the link is rendered as written and a dead one simply ships.
    target: Page with path = this.url and run = this.page.run

    -- An internal notification naming a path that is not a page of this site.
    -- The link still ships, but the maintainer is told, whichever command they
    -- ran. This is the single place the two commands agree on what a dead link
    -- is; they differ only in the shape of the report. A check run records a
    -- warning-severity Finding it can carry to the end and count; a generate
    -- run, which collects nothing, reports it as it passes.
    links_nowhere: kind = internal and target = null

    default_color:
        if kind = plain: config.plain_notification_color
        else: config.linked_notification_color
    color: given_color ?? default_color

    image: SiteImage with reference_key = this.image_reference and run = this.page.run
    image_placement: config.notification_image_placement
}

-- An entry in the site-wide main menu, authored as menu.json at the source
-- root.
entity MenuLink {
    run: GenerationRun
    position: Integer
    url: String
    given_title: String?
    given_icon: String?

    -- An entry pointing off-site is external; anything else names a path
    -- within this site. Classified once, from the url.
    kind: MenuLinkKind

    -- Internal entries point at a page of this site.
    target: Page with path = this.url and run = this.run

    -- Whether the entry can be rendered at all. An external entry always can;
    -- an internal one only when the path it names is a page of this site.
    --
    -- An internal entry naming a path with no page is a typo in menu.json, not
    -- a reason to abandon the run. It is reported to the maintainer and left
    -- out of the published menu; everything else about the run is unaffected.
    -- The published menu therefore only ever contains entries that resolve.
    resolves: kind = external or target != null

    -- An internal entry takes its title and icon from the page it points at
    -- unless the maintainer overrode them. An external entry has no page to
    -- fall back on, so it must supply its own title.
    title: given_title ?? target?.title
    icon: given_icon ?? target?.icon

    -- An internal entry also offers its target's direct children as a dropdown.
    dropdown: target?.ordered_children ?? {}
}

-- A link to the maintainer's presence on another site, rendered site-wide.
entity SocialLink {
    run: GenerationRun
    position: Integer
    url: String
    given_icon: String?
    given_brand_color: String?

    -- Recognised from the url. Classified once, on registration.
    provider: SocialProvider

    -- Recognised providers carry a canonical icon and brand colour. Note this
    -- is a brand colour, not a Bulma semantic Color.
    icon: if provider = other: given_icon else: canonical_icon(provider)
    brand_color: if provider = other: given_brand_color else: canonical_brand_color(provider)
}

-- An image the maintainer listed for publication, authored as images.json in a
-- directory of the image tree. It is published as a set of responsively sized
-- variants that the site can choose between.
entity SiteImage {
    run: GenerationRun

    -- How pages and notifications refer to this image.
    reference_key: String
    source_file: String
    alt_text: String
    given_title: String?
    status: declared | rendered | unavailable

    transitions status {
        declared -> rendered
        declared -> unavailable
        terminal: rendered, unavailable
    }

    source_width: Integer when status = rendered
    source_height: Integer when status = rendered

    -- The closest standard ratio to the source image's own proportions. The
    -- image is centre-cropped to it.
    ratio: ImageRatio when status = rendered

    title: given_title ?? base_name(source_file)

    -- The variants published for this image: the standard widths that fit
    -- within the cropped image, or the image's own width when it is smaller
    -- than every standard width.
    variant_widths: widths_up_to(config.responsive_widths, source_width)

    -- The widest published variant is what a client falls back to when it
    -- cannot choose from the set.
    fallback_width: variant_widths.first
}

-- One problem a check run found. A finding is a record, not an exception: it is
-- collected and carried to the end of the run, so that finding one thing wrong
-- never prevents finding the next.
entity Finding {
    run: GenerationRun
    kind: FindingKind

    -- Where the maintainer should look: a file path, a page path or a url.
    location: String

    -- The offending value, where naming it helps: the reference key, the image
    -- reference, or the content reference that could not be understood.
    detail: String?

    -- Severity is fixed policy, not something a site configures. A finding is an
    -- error when it breaks an invariant this spec states, or when a real
    -- generate run would abort on it; the two agree in spirit. Everything a
    -- generate run tolerates by skipping or degrading and carrying on is a
    -- warning.
    severity:
        if kind in config.error_finding_kinds: config.failing_severity
        else: config.tolerated_severity
}

-- One run of `init`: a short process that turns the maintainer's current
-- directory into a fresh, deployable vados project. Unlike GenerationRun,
-- which reads an existing source and image tree, this run creates one -- there
-- is nothing to read until it has finished, so it is its own lifecycle rather
-- than a third RunMode. There is deliberately no location field: init always
-- operates on the maintainer's current directory, never a path it is given.
entity InitializationRun {
    status: init_requested | conflict_found | scaffolded

    transitions status {
        init_requested -> conflict_found
        init_requested -> scaffolded
        terminal: conflict_found, scaffolded
    }

    -- Checked before a single basic is asked, so the maintainer never answers
    -- questions only to be told at the end that init can't proceed. Every
    -- artifact init would create is checked, and every one already occupied
    -- is reported, not just the first -- the same judgement CheckInvocation
    -- makes, that finding everything in one pass matters more than stopping
    -- at the first problem.
    conflicts: ScaffoldConflict with run = this
    has_conflicts: conflicts.count > 0

    -- The one basic with no default: the maintainer must supply it.
    site_title: String when status = scaffolded

    -- The rest, as given. Null means the maintainer accepted the default.
    given_home_intro: String? when status = scaffolded
    given_primary_color: String? when status = scaffolded
    given_footer_text: String? when status = scaffolded

    -- Resolved values actually written into the scaffold.
    home_intro: given_home_intro ?? default_home_intro_for(site_title)
    primary_color: given_primary_color ?? config.default_primary_color
    footer_text: given_footer_text ?? config.default_footer_text

    -- Opt-in extras, not defaulted basics: a provider the maintainer skipped
    -- is simply not in this list, never filled in with a default handle.
    -- Every entry is a recognised provider; see WizardOnlyGathersRecognizedProviders.
    given_socials: List<SocialHandle> when status = scaffolded

    -- Whether init performed each of these, or found it already done and left
    -- it alone. Exposed so the maintainer knows what happened without having
    -- to go check for themselves.
    repository_initialized: Boolean when status = scaffolded
    gitignore_created: Boolean when status = scaffolded
}

-- An artifact `init` would create whose path was already occupied. Recorded
-- rather than raised, so the conflict check can find every occupied path in
-- one pass instead of stopping at the first.
entity ScaffoldConflict {
    run: InitializationRun
    kind: ScaffoldArtifactKind

    -- Where the maintainer should look to resolve the conflict.
    path: String
}

-- Authoring content into an existing project --------------------------------
--
-- The six entities below are each their own short-lived run, the same way
-- InitializationRun is its own lifecycle rather than a third RunMode: they
-- read and write an existing source/image tree directly, at a point where
-- there is no GenerationRun in progress to attach to. None of them creates a
-- Page, SiteImage, SocialLink, MenuLink or SiteConfig -- those only ever come
-- from a GenerationRun discovering an already-authored tree. Instead each
-- ends by declaring an outcome fact (PageFileWritten, ImageFileRegistered,
-- PageImageAttached, SocialLinkAdded/Updated/Removed, FooterContentUpdated,
-- MenuLinkAppended) that a later `generate` or `check` run would read back.

-- One run of `page new`: creates a page.json (and, unless the maintainer
-- supplies their own content reference, a stub content file) for a path not
-- yet in the source tree. Two-phase for the same reason InitializationRun
-- checks for conflicts before asking for any basics: nothing about a title,
-- subtitle or summary is worth asking for if the page can't be created.
-- Every status enum below spells out its own values in full
-- (page_creation_requested rather than a bare requested) rather than sharing
-- short names like `requested`/`created` across the six entities in this
-- section. Status literals are otherwise scoped per entity -- an inline
-- enum has no type identity outside its own field -- but distinct names
-- also keep any diagnostic or error message about "status requested"
-- unambiguous about which of these six runs it means, the same reason
-- InitializationRun already spells its own first state `init_requested`
-- rather than reusing GenerationRun's `requested`.

entity PageCreationRun {
    source_location: String
    image_source_location: String?
    given_path: String
    given_image_reference: String?
    status: page_creation_requested | page_creation_blocked | page_created

    transitions status {
        page_creation_requested -> page_creation_blocked
        page_creation_requested -> page_created
        terminal: page_creation_blocked, page_created
    }

    blocks: PageCreationBlock with run = this
    is_blocked: blocks.count > 0

    -- Present once the blocker check has passed and the maintainer's
    -- remaining details have arrived.
    title: String when status = page_created
    sub_title: String? when status = page_created
    icon: String? when status = page_created
    summary: String? when status = page_created
    order: Integer? when status = page_created
    given_content: ContentReference? when status = page_created

    -- A page.json with no content reference gets the same fallback a
    -- directory with no page.json gets at generate/check time: a bare
    -- heading. See DiscoverPage.
    content: given_content ?? default_page_content(given_path)
    content_was_defaulted: given_content = null
}

-- One reason a page-creation request couldn't proceed. Recorded rather than
-- raised, so path_already_a_page, parent_path_is_not_a_page and
-- image_reference_unresolved are all found in one pass instead of the
-- maintainer discovering them one `page new` attempt at a time.
entity PageCreationBlock {
    run: PageCreationRun
    reason: PageCreationBlockReason
    detail: String
}

-- One run of `image add`: registers one entry in an images.json, creating the
-- file if the directory has none yet. Two-phase for the same reason as
-- PageCreationRun: a duplicate reference key or an unreadable source file
-- means the registration can never succeed, so there is no reason to ask for
-- a title or alternative text first.
entity ImageRegistrationRun {
    image_source_location: String
    given_dir: String
    given_file_name: String
    status: image_registration_requested | image_registration_blocked | image_registered

    transitions status {
        image_registration_requested -> image_registration_blocked
        image_registration_requested -> image_registered
        terminal: image_registration_blocked, image_registered
    }

    -- How this image will be looked up from a page or notification, derived
    -- the moment the location is known -- before the blocker check even
    -- runs, since reference_key_already_used needs it.
    reference_key: reference_key_for(given_dir, given_file_name)

    blocks: ImageRegistrationBlock with run = this
    is_blocked: blocks.count > 0

    title: String? when status = image_registered
    alt_text: String when status = image_registered
}

entity ImageRegistrationBlock {
    run: ImageRegistrationRun
    reason: ImageRegistrationBlockReason
    detail: String
}

-- One run of `page add-image`: attaches an image -- freshly registered or
-- already declared -- to a page that already exists. The first image
-- attached to a page fills its one hero slot; every one after that (or any
-- call once the hero slot is already filled) becomes a notification instead,
-- since a Page has exactly one hero image but any number of notifications.
-- The maintainer can override the inferred role with given_as, but can never
-- force a second hero image onto a page -- hero_already_present exists to say
-- so rather than silently overwriting the first.
entity PageImageAttachmentRun {
    source_location: String
    image_source_location: String
    given_path: String

    -- Exactly one of these two names the image: a freshly registered one
    -- (dir + file_name) or one already declared elsewhere in the tree. See
    -- PageImageAttachmentNamesExactlyOneImageSource.
    given_dir: String?
    given_file_name: String?
    given_existing_reference: String?

    given_as: PageImageRole?
    status: image_attachment_requested | image_attachment_blocked | image_attached

    transitions status {
        image_attachment_requested -> image_attachment_blocked
        image_attachment_requested -> image_attached
        terminal: image_attachment_blocked, image_attached
    }

    reference_key: given_existing_reference ?? reference_key_for(given_dir, given_file_name)
    hero_available: existing_page_image_reference(source_location, given_path) = null
    effective_role: given_as ?? (if hero_available: hero else: notification)

    blocks: PageImageAttachmentBlock with run = this
    is_blocked: blocks.count > 0

    -- Gathered once the blocker check has passed. given_alt_text is required
    -- only when a new image is being registered (see
    -- PageImageAttachmentRegistersAltTextForNewImages); given_side and
    -- given_caption matter only once effective_role resolves to
    -- notification. The maintainer's actual prompt sequence, which asks for
    -- only the subset that applies, is excluded per this file's own Excludes
    -- section -- every call still passes through this one phase, trivially
    -- when nothing further is needed.
    given_alt_text: String? when status = image_attached
    given_side: NotificationSide? when status = image_attached
    given_caption: String? when status = image_attached

    -- The alternative text the attached image carries, whichever way it was
    -- named: freshly given for a new registration, already on file for a
    -- reused reference.
    resolved_alt_text:
        if given_existing_reference != null:
            declared_image_alt_text(image_source_location, given_existing_reference)
        else:
            given_alt_text
    effective_caption: given_caption ?? resolved_alt_text
}

entity PageImageAttachmentBlock {
    run: PageImageAttachmentRun
    reason: PageImageAttachmentBlockReason
    detail: String
}

-- One run of `social add`, `social update` or `social remove`, unified by
-- change_kind the same way GenerationRun.mode unifies `generate` and `check`.
-- Unlike the three entities above, there is no expensive prompt sequence to
-- protect the maintainer from: matching an existing link and judging
-- completeness are both cheap, so this is single-phase.
--
-- `update` replaces the matched link wholesale rather than merging individual
-- fields into it: the maintainer gives a complete replacement (a handle, or a
-- url with its icon and colour), the same shape `add` accepts for a new one.
-- A partial merge would need to define what "keep the rest as it was" means
-- when only some fields are given, which is more machinery than a rename
-- command needs.
entity SocialLinkChangeRun {
    source_location: String
    change_kind: SocialLinkChangeKind
    status: social_change_requested | social_change_rejected | social_change_applied

    transitions status {
        social_change_requested -> social_change_rejected
        social_change_requested -> social_change_applied
        terminal: social_change_rejected, social_change_applied
    }

    -- add/update: exactly one of these two ways of naming the link.
    given_handle: SocialHandle?
    given_url: String?
    given_icon: String?
    given_brand_color: String?

    -- update/remove: which existing link, and how to disambiguate when more
    -- than one matches.
    given_match: String?
    given_index: Integer?

    resulting_url:
        if given_handle != null: profile_url_for(given_handle.provider, given_handle.handle)
        else: given_url
    resulting_provider: social_provider_of(resulting_url)

    rejection: SocialLinkChangeRejectionReason? when status = social_change_rejected
}

-- One run of `footer set`: replaces main.json's single footer content
-- reference. The simplest of the six -- nothing about a footer content
-- reference can be rejected that ContentReference doesn't already classify
-- for itself (see RunStructuralChecks's handling of an unrecognised
-- reference), so there is no blocked/rejected state to model.
entity FooterUpdateRun {
    source_location: String
    footer_content: ContentReference
    status: footer_update_requested | footer_updated

    transitions status {
        footer_update_requested -> footer_updated
        terminal: footer_updated
    }
}

-- One run of `menu add-item`: appends one entry to menu.json's main menu.
-- Single-phase like SocialLinkChangeRun: nothing about a menu entry is
-- expensive enough to check that it is worth protecting the maintainer from
-- a prompt sequence first. Deliberately add-only -- there is no
-- `menu remove-item`/`menu update-item` yet, the same way PageCreationRun and
-- ImageRegistrationRun have no delete/rename counterpart either (see this
-- file's own Excludes section); this is the open question it closes, and it
-- opens a narrower one of its own at the bottom of this file.
entity MenuLinkAdditionRun {
    source_location: String
    given_url: String
    given_title: String?
    given_icon: String?
    status: menu_link_addition_requested | menu_link_addition_rejected | menu_link_added

    transitions status {
        menu_link_addition_requested -> menu_link_addition_rejected
        menu_link_addition_requested -> menu_link_added
        terminal: menu_link_addition_rejected, menu_link_added
    }

    -- Classified the same way RegisterMainMenuLink classifies a declared
    -- link, so add-time judgement and read-time judgement never drift apart.
    kind: menu_link_kind_of(given_url)

    rejection: MenuLinkAdditionRejectionReason? when status = menu_link_addition_rejected

    -- Whether this entry, once added, would actually show up in the
    -- published menu. Exposed on the outcome rather than blocked on: an
    -- internal link naming a path with no page yet is a legitimate forward
    -- reference (see MenuLink.resolves), not a mistake `add-item` should
    -- refuse to write.
    would_resolve: kind = external or page_exists_at(source_location, given_url)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- Bulma defaults applied when the maintainer configures nothing.
    default_navbar_color: Color = warning
    default_background_class: String = "has-background-light"
    default_css_links: List<String> = [
        "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@mdi/font@6.5.95/css/materialdesignicons.min.css",
        "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bulma@0.9.3/css/bulma.min.css"
    ]
    default_js_links: List<String> = [ "/js/vados.js" ]

    -- Notification colouring.
    plain_notification_color: Color = info
    linked_notification_color: Color = link
    side_notification_color: Color = info

    -- Navigation thresholds, in path segments.
    breadcrumb_min_depth: Integer = 2
    side_menu_min_depth: Integer = 3

    -- How many neighbouring pages a page advertises.
    max_side_notifications: Integer = 3

    -- Where each kind of image sits, which fixes its loading priority and
    -- display size. Fixed policy rather than something a site can change.
    hero_image_placement: ImagePlacement = main
    notification_image_placement: ImagePlacement = sub
    side_notification_image_placement: ImagePlacement = side

    -- Responsive variant widths, widest first.
    responsive_widths: List<Integer> = [ 1972, 1479, 986, 632, 425, 318, 159 ]

    -- Which findings fail a check. Fixed policy rather than something a site
    -- can change: each of these either breaks an invariant this spec states, or
    -- is a condition a real generate run cannot get past. Every other kind is a
    -- warning, because generation tolerates it by skipping or degrading.
    error_finding_kinds: Set<FindingKind> = {
        site_config_unreadable,
        menu_unreadable,
        page_config_unreadable,
        unrecognised_content_reference,
        external_menu_link_without_title,
        unrecognised_social_link_incomplete,
        duplicate_image_reference,
        image_without_alternative_text
    }

    -- The two severities themselves. Neither is adjustable; they are named here
    -- so that the classification above has somewhere to land.
    failing_severity: FindingSeverity = error
    tolerated_severity: FindingSeverity = warning

    -- init's defaults, applied when the maintainer accepts a basic as given
    -- rather than typing one. Bulma's own stock primary colour, so accepting
    -- every default leaves an unmodified Bulma look.
    default_primary_color: String = "#00d1b2"
    default_footer_text: String = "Built with vados."

    -- Every artifact init creates in one run. Fixed policy, not something a
    -- site configures: this is the exact set the conflict check tests.
    scaffold_artifact_kinds: Set<ScaffoldArtifactKind> = {
        site_config,
        menu_config,
        home_page,
        image_tree,
        stylesheet_entry,
        package_manifest,
        netlify_config,
        readme
    }

    -- The providers init prompts a handle for, one skippable prompt each.
    -- `other` is deliberately excluded: it has no canonical icon or colour,
    -- so a quick prompt for a bare handle could never satisfy
    -- UnrecognisedSocialLinkIsFullyDescribed.
    recognized_social_providers: Set<SocialProvider> = {
        github, linked_in, facebook, youtube, twitter
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Starting a run -----------------------------------------------------------

rule BeginGeneration {
    when: GenerateRequested(source, image_source, destination)
    ensures: GenerationRun.created(
        mode: generate,
        source_location: source,
        image_source_location: image_source,
        destination: destination,
        status: requested
    )
}

-- A check run is begun exactly like a generate run, minus the destination.
-- Everything up to the point where the structure is known is the same work.
rule BeginCheck {
    when: CheckRequested(source, image_source)
    ensures: GenerationRun.created(
        mode: check,
        source_location: source,
        image_source_location: image_source,
        status: requested
    )
}

rule AdoptSiteConfiguration {
    when: SiteConfigurationDeclared(
        run, site_title, footer_content,
        extra_css_files, extra_js_files,
        include_default_css?, include_default_js?,
        background_class?, navbar_color?
    )
    requires: run.status = requested
    ensures:
        SiteConfig.created(
            run: run,
            site_title: site_title,
            footer_content: footer_content,
            extra_css_files: extra_css_files,
            extra_js_files: extra_js_files,
            include_default_css: include_default_css,
            include_default_js: include_default_js,
            background_class: background_class,
            navbar_color: navbar_color
        )
        run.status = configured
}

-- The generator ships its own script for the burger menu and side menu
-- toggles. When the default script is in use it is published alongside the
-- site so pages can link to it.
rule PublishDefaultBehaviourScript {
    when: site_config: SiteConfig.created
    requires: site_config.run.mode = generate
    requires: site_config.default_js_included
    ensures: ScriptAssetPublished(
        run: site_config.run,
        location: config.default_js_links.first
    )
}

-- Menus --------------------------------------------------------------------

rule RegisterMainMenuLink {
    when: MainMenuLinkDeclared(run, position, url, title?, icon?)
    requires: run.status = configured
    ensures: MenuLink.created(
        run: run,
        position: position,
        url: url,
        kind: menu_link_kind_of(url),
        given_title: title,
        given_icon: icon
    )
}

rule RegisterSocialLink {
    when: SocialLinkDeclared(run, position, url, icon?, brand_color?)
    requires: run.status = configured
    ensures: SocialLink.created(
        run: run,
        position: position,
        url: url,
        provider: social_provider_of(url),
        given_icon: icon,
        given_brand_color: brand_color
    )
}

-- Content structure --------------------------------------------------------

-- Every directory under the source root becomes a page. A directory without a
-- page.json still becomes a page: it is titled after the directory and its
-- content is a bare heading.
rule DiscoverPage {
    when: PageDeclared(
        run, path, title?, sub_title?, image?, icon?, summary?, content?, order?
    )
    requires: run.status = configured
    let parent = Page{run: run, path: parent_path(path)}
    ensures: Page.created(
        run: run,
        path: path,
        parent: parent,
        title: title ?? directory_name(path),
        content: content ?? default_page_content(path),
        sub_title: sub_title,
        image_reference: image,
        icon: icon,
        summary: summary,
        order: order,
        status: discovered
    )
}

rule AttachNotification {
    when: PageNotificationDeclared(
        page, side, position, content, title?, image?, url?, color?
    )
    requires: page.status = discovered
    ensures: Notification.created(
        page: page,
        side: side,
        position: position,
        content: content,
        kind: notification_kind_of(url),
        title: title,
        image_reference: image,
        url: url,
        given_color: color
    )
}

rule CompleteSourceScan {
    when: SourceScanCompleted(run)
    requires: run.status = configured
    ensures: run.status = structure_built
}

-- Images -------------------------------------------------------------------

rule DeclareImage {
    when: ImageDeclared(run, reference_key, source_file, alt_text, title?)
    requires: run.status = configured
    ensures: SiteImage.created(
        run: run,
        reference_key: reference_key,
        source_file: source_file,
        alt_text: alt_text,
        given_title: title,
        status: declared
    )
}

-- A declared image is cropped to the closest standard aspect ratio and
-- published as a set of responsively sized variants.
rule RenderImageVariants {
    when: image: SiteImage.created
    requires: image.run.mode = generate
    requires: image.status = declared
    requires: source_readable(image.source_file)
    let width = source_width_of(image.source_file)
    let height = source_height_of(image.source_file)
    ensures:
        image.source_width = width
        image.source_height = height
        image.ratio = best_fitting_ratio(width, height)
        image.status = rendered
        ImageVariantsPublished(
            run: image.run,
            image: image,
            widths: widths_up_to(config.responsive_widths, width)
        )

    @guidance
        -- Variants are content-addressed by image and width, so a variant that
        -- is already present at the destination is reused rather than
        -- regenerated. This makes repeat runs cheap.
}

-- An image that cannot be read is skipped: the run continues and pages that
-- referred to it are published without it.
--
-- This is deliberate. A single corrupt, truncated or unsupported file must not
-- stop the rest of the site from reaching the destination; the page that named
-- it simply publishes without its image. The image is left in a distinct
-- `unavailable` state rather than being discarded, so the failure is visible
-- and reportable instead of looking like an image that was never declared.
--
-- This runs in both modes, unlike RenderImageVariants. Asking whether a file is
-- there is cheap and reads no image bytes, so a check run asks it too and
-- reports what it learns; decoding, cropping and resizing are generate-only.
rule SkipUnreadableImage {
    when: image: SiteImage.created
    requires: image.status = declared
    requires: not source_readable(image.source_file)
    ensures: image.status = unavailable
}

-- Publication --------------------------------------------------------------

-- Nothing can be assembled until the whole structure is known, because a page
-- shows its neighbours, its ancestors and the site-wide menus.
rule PublishSite {
    when: site: GenerationRun.status transitions_to structure_built
    requires: site.mode = generate
    ensures:
        for page in site.pages:
            PageAssemblyRequested(page: page)
        for link in site.main_menu_links where not link.resolves:
            MainMenuLinkDropped(run: site, link: link, url: link.url)

        -- A notification pointing at a path with no page. Unlike a dropped menu
        -- entry, the link is still published exactly as written: the maintainer
        -- is told about it, not protected from it. Reported here rather than
        -- collected, because a generate run keeps no findings.
        for n in site.notifications where n.links_nowhere:
            DeadNotificationLinkDetected(run: site, notification: n, url: n.url)

        site.status = published
}

-- Variants already at the destination are reused rather than regenerated, so
-- the destination accumulates output across runs. Once the run knows the full
-- set of images the maintainer declared, anything at the destination belonging
-- to an image that is no longer declared is removed, so the published site
-- carries exactly the images it claims to and nothing more.
rule PruneOrphanedImageVariants {
    when: site: GenerationRun.status transitions_to published
    ensures: OrphanedVariantsPruned(run: site, declared_images: site.images)
}

-- A page is assembled from its own title, subtitle, summary, content and hero
-- image, plus the site-wide navigation, its breadcrumbs and side menu where
-- they apply, its inherited notifications, its side notifications, the
-- site-wide footer, and the resolved stylesheet and script lists.
rule PublishPage {
    when: PageAssemblyRequested(page)
    requires: page.status = discovered
    ensures:
        page.status = published
        PagePublished(page: page, location: page.output_location)
}

-- Checking -----------------------------------------------------------------

-- Something in the tree could not be read as what it claims to be: main.json,
-- menu.json, a page.json or an images.json. Whatever reads the tree reports it
-- rather than raising it, so reading carries on with the rest of the tree and
-- the maintainer learns about every unreadable file at once.
rule RecordSourceProblem {
    when: SourceProblemDetected(run, kind, location, detail?)
    requires: run.mode = check

    -- Unreadable site configuration is the one problem that could strand the
    -- run before discovery even starts, leaving the maintainer told about that
    -- single file and nothing else. The run carries on without site
    -- configuration instead, so the rest of the tree is still walked and still
    -- reported on. Everything downstream copes with a run that has none.
    --
    -- Carrying on is deliberate, and the partial report it produces is the
    -- point rather than a shortcoming. Finding everything in one pass matters
    -- more than stopping at the first problem, and that holds for the very
    -- first file as much as for any other: a report saying only "main.json is
    -- unreadable" costs the maintainer a whole extra round trip before they
    -- learn anything about the rest of the tree. What cannot be checked without
    -- site configuration -- the title, footer and navbar -- is simply not
    -- checked; everything else still is.
    let stranded = kind = site_config_unreadable and run.status = requested

    ensures:
        Finding.created(
            run: run,
            kind: kind,
            location: location,
            detail: detail
        )
        if stranded:
            run.status = configured
}

-- The check pass. It runs once the whole structure is known, for the same
-- reason generation waits: a menu entry, a page image and a notification link
-- can only be resolved against pages and images that have all been declared.
--
-- Every clause below is asserted together. There is no order between them and
-- no clause can prevent another from being evaluated, which is the whole point:
-- a check reports everything wrong with the tree, not the first thing wrong.
-- Nothing here writes to a destination; the run ends at `checked`, never
-- `published`.
rule RunStructuralChecks {
    when: run: GenerationRun.status transitions_to structure_built
    requires: run.mode = check

    let footer = run.site_config?.footer_content

    ensures:
        -- Errors: each of these either breaks a stated invariant or is a
        -- condition a real generate run would abort on.

        for l in run.main_menu_links where l.kind = external and l.given_title = null:
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: external_menu_link_without_title,
                location: l.url
            )

        for s in run.social_links
            where s.provider = other and (s.given_icon = null or s.given_brand_color = null):
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: unrecognised_social_link_incomplete,
                location: s.url
            )

        for p in run.pages where p.content.kind = unrecognised:
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: unrecognised_content_reference,
                location: p.path,
                detail: p.content.reference
            )

        for n in run.notifications where n.content.kind = unrecognised:
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: unrecognised_content_reference,
                location: n.page.path,
                detail: n.content.reference
            )

        if footer != null and footer.kind = unrecognised:
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: unrecognised_content_reference,
                location: run.source_location,
                detail: footer.reference
            )

        -- Two images claiming the same reference key: the later silently
        -- displaces the earlier, so pages get an image nobody meant to give
        -- them. Both sides of the collision are reported.
        for i in run.images
            where run.images.any(o => o != i and o.reference_key = i.reference_key):
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: duplicate_image_reference,
                location: i.source_file,
                detail: i.reference_key
            )

        for i in run.images where i.alt_text = "":
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: image_without_alternative_text,
                location: i.source_file,
                detail: i.reference_key
            )

        -- Warnings: each of these is something generation already tolerates by
        -- dropping or degrading, so it is reported but does not fail the check.

        for l in run.main_menu_links where not l.resolves:
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: unresolved_menu_link,
                location: l.url
            )

        for p in run.pages where p.image_reference != null and p.main_image = null:
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: unknown_page_image_reference,
                location: p.path,
                detail: p.image_reference
            )

        for n in run.notifications where n.image_reference != null and n.image = null:
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: unknown_notification_image_reference,
                location: n.page.path,
                detail: n.image_reference
            )

        -- A notification pointing at a path with no page. Generation reports
        -- the same thing about the same notifications, from the same
        -- Notification.links_nowhere; the difference is only that a check run
        -- keeps its report, so it becomes a Finding that can be counted and
        -- listed. The link ships either way.
        for n in run.notifications where n.links_nowhere:
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: dead_internal_notification_link,
                location: n.page.path,
                detail: n.url
            )

        for p in run.pages
            where p.content.kind in {markdown_file, html_file}
                and not source_readable(p.content.reference):
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: missing_content_file,
                location: p.path,
                detail: p.content.reference
            )

        for n in run.notifications
            where n.content.kind in {markdown_file, html_file}
                and not source_readable(n.content.reference):
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: missing_content_file,
                location: n.page.path,
                detail: n.content.reference
            )

        if footer != null
            and footer.kind in {markdown_file, html_file}
            and not source_readable(footer.reference):
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: missing_content_file,
                location: run.source_location,
                detail: footer.reference
            )

        for i in run.images where i.status = unavailable:
            Finding.created(
                run: run,
                kind: missing_image_source_file,
                location: i.source_file,
                detail: i.reference_key
            )

        run.status = checked

    @guidance
        -- Structural only. A check asks whether a file is there, whether a
        -- reference resolves and whether a declaration is complete. It never
        -- decodes an image, so it stays cheap enough to run on every save.
}

-- The outcome of a check is a report, not a site. It carries every finding and
-- the two counts; whether the check passed follows from the error count alone.
rule ReportCheckOutcome {
    when: run: GenerationRun.status transitions_to checked
    ensures: CheckOutcomeReported(
        run: run,
        findings: run.findings,
        error_count: run.error_count,
        warning_count: run.warning_count,
        passed: run.check_passed
    )
}

-- Initializing a project ----------------------------------------------------

rule BeginInit {
    when: InitRequested()
    ensures: InitializationRun.created(status: init_requested)
}

-- Every artifact init would create is checked in one pass, immediately, before
-- the maintainer is asked anything. A conflict-free run is left at
-- `init_requested` rather than advanced further here: scaffolding itself only
-- happens once the basics arrive, in ScaffoldProject.
rule DetectScaffoldConflicts {
    when: run: InitializationRun.created
    ensures:
        for kind in config.scaffold_artifact_kinds where artifact_path_occupied(kind):
            ScaffoldConflict.created(
                run: run,
                kind: kind,
                path: artifact_path(kind)
            )
        if run.has_conflicts:
            run.status = conflict_found
}

-- Scaffolding happens once, from the whole bundle of basics at once -- the
-- same choice SiteConfigurationDeclared makes for main.json's fields, rather
-- than one trigger per prompt. `requires: run.status = init_requested` is
-- what makes this unreachable for a run the conflict check already rejected.
--
-- Version control is settled in this same rule rather than a separate one
-- reacting to `transitions_to scaffolded`: repository_initialized and
-- gitignore_created are when-qualified on `scaffolded`, and the obligation to
-- set a field entering a `when` set falls on whichever rule produces the
-- transition -- the same reason RenderImageVariants sets source_width,
-- source_height and ratio itself rather than leaving them to a later rule.
rule ScaffoldProject {
    when: ProjectBasicsProvided(
        run, site_title, home_intro?, primary_color?, footer_text?, socials
    )
    requires: run.status = init_requested
    ensures:
        run.site_title = site_title
        run.given_home_intro = home_intro
        run.given_primary_color = primary_color
        run.given_footer_text = footer_text
        run.given_socials = socials
        run.repository_initialized = not git_repository_present()
        run.gitignore_created = not gitignore_present()
        run.status = scaffolded
        ProjectScaffolded(run: run, kinds: config.scaffold_artifact_kinds)
        if run.repository_initialized:
            RepositoryInitialized(run: run)
        if run.gitignore_created:
            GitignoreCreated(run: run)
        else:
            GitignoreEntriesMerged(run: run)
}

-- Authoring content into an existing project --------------------------------

-- Creating a page ------------------------------------------------------

rule RequestPageCreation {
    when: PageCreationRequested(source, image_source?, path, image?)
    ensures: PageCreationRun.created(
        source_location: source,
        image_source_location: image_source,
        given_path: path,
        given_image_reference: image,
        status: page_creation_requested
    )
}

-- Every blocker is checked in one pass, immediately, before the maintainer is
-- asked for a title or anything else -- the same judgement
-- DetectScaffoldConflicts makes for `init`.
rule DetectPageCreationBlockers {
    when: run: PageCreationRun.created
    let parent = parent_path(run.given_path)
    ensures:
        if page_exists_at(run.source_location, run.given_path):
            PageCreationBlock.created(
                run: run, reason: path_already_a_page, detail: run.given_path
            )
        if parent != null and not page_exists_at(run.source_location, parent):
            PageCreationBlock.created(
                run: run, reason: parent_path_is_not_a_page, detail: parent
            )
        if run.given_image_reference != null
            and not image_reference_resolves(run.image_source_location, run.given_image_reference):
            PageCreationBlock.created(
                run: run, reason: image_reference_unresolved, detail: run.given_image_reference
            )
        if run.is_blocked:
            run.status = page_creation_blocked
}

rule ProvidePageCreationDetails {
    when: PageCreationDetailsProvided(run, title, sub_title?, icon?, summary?, order?, content?)
    requires: run.status = page_creation_requested
    ensures:
        run.title = title
        run.sub_title = sub_title
        run.icon = icon
        run.summary = summary
        run.order = order
        run.given_content = content
        run.status = page_created
        PageFileWritten(
            run: run,
            path: run.given_path,
            title: title,
            content: content ?? default_page_content(run.given_path),
            sub_title: sub_title,
            icon: icon,
            summary: summary,
            order: order,
            image_reference: run.given_image_reference
        )
}

-- Registering an image ---------------------------------------------------

rule RequestImageRegistration {
    when: ImageRegistrationRequested(image_source, dir, file_name)
    ensures: ImageRegistrationRun.created(
        image_source_location: image_source,
        given_dir: dir,
        given_file_name: file_name,
        status: image_registration_requested
    )
}

rule DetectImageRegistrationBlockers {
    when: run: ImageRegistrationRun.created
    ensures:
        if not source_readable(
            image_source_path(run.image_source_location, run.given_dir, run.given_file_name)
        ):
            ImageRegistrationBlock.created(
                run: run, reason: source_file_unreadable, detail: run.given_file_name
            )
        if image_reference_key_used(run.image_source_location, run.reference_key):
            ImageRegistrationBlock.created(
                run: run, reason: reference_key_already_used, detail: run.reference_key
            )
        if run.is_blocked:
            run.status = image_registration_blocked
}

rule ProvideImageRegistrationDetails {
    when: ImageRegistrationDetailsProvided(run, title?, alt_text)
    requires: run.status = image_registration_requested
    ensures:
        run.title = title
        run.alt_text = alt_text
        run.status = image_registered
        ImageFileRegistered(
            run: run,
            reference_key: run.reference_key,
            source_file: image_source_path(run.image_source_location, run.given_dir, run.given_file_name),
            alt_text: alt_text,
            title: title ?? base_name(run.given_file_name)
        )
}

-- Attaching an image to a page -------------------------------------------

rule RequestPageImageAttachment {
    when: PageImageAttachmentRequested(
        source, image_source, path, dir?, file_name?, existing_reference?, as?
    )
    ensures: PageImageAttachmentRun.created(
        source_location: source,
        image_source_location: image_source,
        given_path: path,
        given_dir: dir,
        given_file_name: file_name,
        given_existing_reference: existing_reference,
        given_as: as,
        status: image_attachment_requested
    )
}

rule DetectPageImageAttachmentBlockers {
    when: run: PageImageAttachmentRun.created
    ensures:
        if not page_exists_at(run.source_location, run.given_path):
            PageImageAttachmentBlock.created(
                run: run, reason: target_page_missing, detail: run.given_path
            )
        if run.given_existing_reference = null
            and not source_readable(
                image_source_path(run.image_source_location, run.given_dir, run.given_file_name)
            ):
            PageImageAttachmentBlock.created(
                run: run, reason: source_file_unreadable, detail: run.given_file_name
            )
        if run.given_existing_reference = null
            and image_reference_key_used(run.image_source_location, run.reference_key):
            PageImageAttachmentBlock.created(
                run: run, reason: reference_key_already_used, detail: run.reference_key
            )
        if run.given_existing_reference != null
            and not image_reference_resolves(run.image_source_location, run.given_existing_reference):
            PageImageAttachmentBlock.created(
                run: run, reason: image_reference_unresolved, detail: run.given_existing_reference
            )
        if run.given_as = hero and not run.hero_available:
            PageImageAttachmentBlock.created(
                run: run, reason: hero_already_present, detail: run.given_path
            )
        if run.is_blocked:
            run.status = image_attachment_blocked
}

-- Always the mechanism that reaches `created`, even when effective_role
-- resolves to hero and there is nothing left to gather beyond a new image's
-- own alternative text: this keeps exactly one rule producing the
-- `created` transition rather than two convergent ones.
rule ProvidePageImageAttachmentDetails {
    when: PageImageAttachmentDetailsProvided(run, alt_text?, side?, caption?)
    requires: run.status = image_attachment_requested
    ensures:
        run.given_alt_text = alt_text
        run.given_side = side
        run.given_caption = caption
        run.status = image_attached
        PageImageAttached(
            run: run,
            path: run.given_path,
            role: run.effective_role,
            reference_key: run.reference_key,
            side: if run.effective_role = notification: run.given_side ?? right else: null,
            content: if run.effective_role = notification: run.effective_caption else: null
        )
}

-- Changing a social link --------------------------------------------------

rule RequestSocialLinkAddition {
    when: SocialLinkAdditionRequested(source, handle?, url?, icon?, brand_color?)
    ensures: SocialLinkChangeRun.created(
        source_location: source,
        change_kind: add,
        given_handle: handle,
        given_url: url,
        given_icon: icon,
        given_brand_color: brand_color,
        status: social_change_requested
    )
}

rule RequestSocialLinkUpdate {
    when: SocialLinkUpdateRequested(source, match, index?, handle?, url?, icon?, brand_color?)
    ensures: SocialLinkChangeRun.created(
        source_location: source,
        change_kind: update,
        given_match: match,
        given_index: index,
        given_handle: handle,
        given_url: url,
        given_icon: icon,
        given_brand_color: brand_color,
        status: social_change_requested
    )
}

rule RequestSocialLinkRemoval {
    when: SocialLinkRemovalRequested(source, match, index?)
    ensures: SocialLinkChangeRun.created(
        source_location: source,
        change_kind: remove,
        given_match: match,
        given_index: index,
        status: social_change_requested
    )
}

-- Guarded on change_kind = add, distinctly from the update/remove rules
-- below, so the three never overlap on the same SocialLinkChangeRun.created
-- trigger.
rule ApplySocialLinkAddition {
    when: run: SocialLinkChangeRun.created
    requires: run.status = social_change_requested
    requires: run.change_kind = add
    ensures:
        if run.resulting_provider = other and (run.given_icon = null or run.given_brand_color = null):
            run.rejection = unrecognised_provider_incomplete
            run.status = social_change_rejected
        else if social_link_url_registered(run.source_location, run.resulting_url):
            run.rejection = url_already_registered
            run.status = social_change_rejected
        else:
            run.status = social_change_applied
            SocialLinkAdded(
                run: run,
                url: run.resulting_url,
                provider: run.resulting_provider,
                icon: run.given_icon,
                brand_color: run.given_brand_color
            )
}

rule ApplySocialLinkUpdate {
    when: run: SocialLinkChangeRun.created
    requires: run.status = social_change_requested
    requires: run.change_kind = update
    let match_count = matching_social_link_count(run.source_location, run.given_match)
    ensures:
        if match_count = 0:
            run.rejection = no_matching_social_link
            run.status = social_change_rejected
        else if match_count > 1 and run.given_index = null:
            run.rejection = ambiguous_social_link_match
            run.status = social_change_rejected
        else if run.given_index != null and (run.given_index < 0 or run.given_index >= match_count):
            run.rejection = match_index_out_of_range
            run.status = social_change_rejected
        else if run.resulting_provider = other and (run.given_icon = null or run.given_brand_color = null):
            run.rejection = unrecognised_provider_incomplete
            run.status = social_change_rejected
        else:
            run.status = social_change_applied
            SocialLinkUpdated(
                run: run,
                previous_url: selected_social_link_url(run.source_location, run.given_match, run.given_index),
                url: run.resulting_url,
                provider: run.resulting_provider,
                icon: run.given_icon,
                brand_color: run.given_brand_color
            )
}

rule ApplySocialLinkRemoval {
    when: run: SocialLinkChangeRun.created
    requires: run.status = social_change_requested
    requires: run.change_kind = remove
    let match_count = matching_social_link_count(run.source_location, run.given_match)
    ensures:
        if match_count = 0:
            run.rejection = no_matching_social_link
            run.status = social_change_rejected
        else if match_count > 1 and run.given_index = null:
            run.rejection = ambiguous_social_link_match
            run.status = social_change_rejected
        else if run.given_index != null and (run.given_index < 0 or run.given_index >= match_count):
            run.rejection = match_index_out_of_range
            run.status = social_change_rejected
        else:
            run.status = social_change_applied
            SocialLinkRemoved(
                run: run,
                url: selected_social_link_url(run.source_location, run.given_match, run.given_index)
            )
}

-- Updating the footer -------------------------------------------------------

rule RequestFooterUpdate {
    when: FooterUpdateRequested(source, footer_content)
    ensures: FooterUpdateRun.created(
        source_location: source,
        footer_content: footer_content,
        status: footer_update_requested
    )
}

-- Nothing here can be rejected (see FooterUpdateRun's own comment), so this
-- fires unconditionally the moment the run exists -- the same two-rule shape
-- DetectPageCreationBlockers/ProvidePageCreationDetails use, just without a
-- blocked branch.
rule ApplyFooterUpdate {
    when: run: FooterUpdateRun.created
    requires: run.status = footer_update_requested
    ensures:
        run.status = footer_updated
        FooterContentUpdated(run: run, footer_content: run.footer_content)
}

-- Appending a main menu entry -----------------------------------------------

rule RequestMenuLinkAddition {
    when: MenuLinkAdditionRequested(source, url, title?, icon?)
    ensures: MenuLinkAdditionRun.created(
        source_location: source,
        given_url: url,
        given_title: title,
        given_icon: icon,
        status: menu_link_addition_requested
    )
}

-- The two ways this can be rejected mirror ApplySocialLinkAddition's shape:
-- one that would break a stated invariant (ExternalMenuLinkCarriesTitle),
-- one that would just duplicate an entry already there.
rule ApplyMenuLinkAddition {
    when: run: MenuLinkAdditionRun.created
    requires: run.status = menu_link_addition_requested
    ensures:
        if run.kind = external and run.given_title = null:
            run.rejection = external_link_without_title
            run.status = menu_link_addition_rejected
        else if menu_link_url_registered(run.source_location, run.given_url):
            run.rejection = url_already_in_menu
            run.status = menu_link_addition_rejected
        else:
            run.status = menu_link_added
            MenuLinkAppended(
                run: run,
                url: run.given_url,
                title: run.given_title,
                icon: run.given_icon
            )
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- An external main menu entry has no page to take a title from, so it must
-- carry one.
invariant ExternalMenuLinkCarriesTitle {
    for l in MenuLinks:
        l.kind = external implies l.given_title != null
}

-- An internal main menu entry pointing at a path with no page is deliberately
-- NOT an invariant violation. It is a valid, if unusual, transient state: the
-- entry is reported and filtered out of the published menu (see
-- MenuLink.resolves and GenerationRun.published_main_menu_links) rather than
-- failing the run.

-- Recognised social providers supply their own icon and brand colour; an
-- unrecognised one must be given both.
invariant UnrecognisedSocialLinkIsFullyDescribed {
    for s in SocialLinks:
        s.provider = other implies s.given_icon != null and s.given_brand_color != null
}

-- Every image carries alternative text; this is an accessibility commitment,
-- not a convenience.
invariant EveryImageHasAlternativeText {
    for i in SiteImages:
        i.alt_text != ""
}

-- Page paths identify pages within a run.
invariant PagePathIsUnique {
    for a in Pages:
        for b in Pages:
            a != b and a.run = b.run implies a.path != b.path
}

-- Image references identify images within a run.
invariant ImageReferenceIsUnique {
    for a in SiteImages:
        for b in SiteImages:
            a != b and a.run = b.run implies a.reference_key != b.reference_key
}

-- Exactly one page has no parent: the site root.
invariant SingleRootPage {
    for a in Pages:
        for b in Pages:
            a != b and a.run = b.run and a.is_root implies not b.is_root
}

-- A page never advertises itself among its side notifications.
invariant SideNotificationsExcludeSelf {
    for p in Pages:
        not (p in p.side_notification_pages)
}

-- A generate run has somewhere to put the site it builds.
invariant GenerateRunHasDestination {
    for r in GenerationRuns:
        r.mode = generate implies r.destination != null
}

-- A check run writes nothing, and is not even told where output would go. This
-- is the structural guarantee behind the promise that a failed check leaves the
-- destination exactly as it was: there is no destination to partially write.
invariant CheckRunHasNoDestination {
    for r in GenerationRuns:
        r.mode = check implies r.destination = null
}

-- Findings belong to check runs. Generation reports its own problems as it goes
-- and does not collect them.
invariant FindingsBelongToCheckRuns {
    for f in Findings:
        f.run.mode = check
}

-- A conflict is only ever recorded for a run the conflict check went on to
-- reject; a scaffolded run never carries one.
invariant ScaffoldConflictsBelongToRejectedRuns {
    for c in ScaffoldConflicts:
        c.run.status = conflict_found
}

-- Nothing is partially written: a run that reaches `scaffolded` had no
-- conflicts, and a run with any conflict never reaches `scaffolded`.
invariant ScaffoldingRequiresNoConflicts {
    for r in InitializationRuns:
        r.status = scaffolded implies r.conflicts.count = 0
}

-- The wizard only ever offers a handle for a provider with a canonical icon
-- and colour; it never gathers one for `other`, which has neither.
invariant WizardOnlyGathersRecognizedProviders {
    for r in InitializationRuns:
        r.status = scaffolded implies
            r.given_socials.all(h => h.provider in config.recognized_social_providers)
}

-- A blocker is only ever recorded for a run the blocker check went on to
-- reject; a created run never carries one. Mirrors
-- ScaffoldConflictsBelongToRejectedRuns for InitializationRun.
invariant PageCreationBlocksBelongToBlockedRuns {
    for b in PageCreationBlocks:
        b.run.status = page_creation_blocked
}

invariant ImageRegistrationBlocksBelongToBlockedRuns {
    for b in ImageRegistrationBlocks:
        b.run.status = image_registration_blocked
}

invariant PageImageAttachmentBlocksBelongToBlockedRuns {
    for b in PageImageAttachmentBlocks:
        b.run.status = image_attachment_blocked
}

-- Exactly one of the two ways of naming an image is given: a freshly
-- registered file, or an already-declared reference.
invariant PageImageAttachmentNamesExactlyOneImageSource {
    for r in PageImageAttachmentRuns:
        (r.given_existing_reference != null
            implies (r.given_dir = null and r.given_file_name = null))
        and (r.given_existing_reference = null
            implies (r.given_dir != null and r.given_file_name != null))
}

-- A page-image attachment never registers a new image without the
-- alternative text EveryImageHasAlternativeText will go on to require of it
-- once a generate/check run reads the tree back.
invariant PageImageAttachmentRegistersAltTextForNewImages {
    for r in PageImageAttachmentRuns:
        r.status = image_attached and r.given_existing_reference = null
            implies r.given_alt_text != null
}

-- A rejected social-link change always carries the one reason it was
-- rejected; an applied one never does.
invariant SocialLinkChangeRejectionMatchesStatus {
    for r in SocialLinkChangeRuns:
        r.status = social_change_rejected implies r.rejection != null
}

-- A rejected menu-link addition always carries the one reason it was
-- rejected; an added one never does.
invariant MenuLinkAdditionRejectionMatchesStatus {
    for r in MenuLinkAdditionRuns:
        r.status = menu_link_addition_rejected implies r.rejection != null
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The `generate` subcommand: source tree, image tree, destination.
surface GeneratorInvocation {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        GenerateRequested(source, image_source, destination)

    @guarantee OneShotRun
        -- Generation is a batch process. A run reads the source and image
        -- trees as they are at the moment it starts and publishes a complete
        -- site; nothing keeps running afterwards.
}

-- The `check` subcommand: source tree and image tree, and no destination,
-- because a check produces no output. It is the pre-flight pass the maintainer
-- runs before generating, and it is meant to be run often.
surface CheckInvocation {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        CheckRequested(source, image_source)

    @guarantee EveryProblemInOnePass
        -- A check reports every problem it can see in a single pass. One
        -- unreadable file, unresolved reference or incomplete declaration never
        -- stops the check from examining the rest of the tree, so the
        -- maintainer gets the whole list at once rather than one problem per
        -- run. This is the reason the command exists.

    @guarantee CheckWritesNothing
        -- A check never writes anything anywhere, not even partially. It is
        -- given no destination, so there is nothing for it to write to.

    @guarantee ExitCodeReflectsErrorsOnly
        -- The command exits non-zero if and only if at least one error-severity
        -- finding was made. Warnings are always reported, and never affect the
        -- exit code or whether the check passed.

    @guarantee CheckIsStructuralOnly
        -- A check resolves references and asks whether files are present. It
        -- never decodes, crops or resizes an image, and never inspects image
        -- bytes for any reason.
}

-- What the maintainer gets back from a check: the whole list, plus the counts
-- and the verdict.
surface CheckReport {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: GenerationRun where mode = check and status = checked

    exposes:
        run.source_location
        run.image_source_location
        run.check_passed
        run.error_count
        run.warning_count

        for f in run.findings:
            f.severity
            f.kind
            f.location
            f.detail

    @guarantee WarningsAreReportedButNeverFail
        -- Warnings are shown with the same prominence as errors and always
        -- appear in the report. They describe things generation tolerates by
        -- dropping or degrading, so they never make the check fail.
}

-- The `init` subcommand: scaffolds a fresh, deployable vados project. Unlike
-- `generate`/`check`, it takes no source or image tree argument -- there is
-- nothing to read yet, and nowhere but the current directory to create one.
surface InitInvocation {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        InitRequested()

    @guarantee ScaffoldsInPlace
        -- init always scaffolds into the maintainer's current directory.
        -- There is no separate "create a new directory" mode.

    @guarantee NoRustCrateIsScaffolded
        -- init writes no Cargo.toml or main.rs. The scaffolded project relies
        -- on the `vados` binary being installed separately -- the same binary
        -- GeneratorInvocation and CheckInvocation are surfaces of.

    @guarantee DeployableWithoutFurtherSetup
        -- The scaffolded project is deployable to Netlify by connecting the
        -- repository alone: its Netlify build configuration installs Rust and
        -- `vados`, builds the stylesheet, and runs `generate`.

    @guarantee AlwaysInstallsCurrentVados
        -- The scaffolded Netlify build configuration installs whatever
        -- version of `vados` is current at deploy time, not a pinned one:
        -- deploys pick up vados fixes automatically. A considered trade
        -- against reproducibility, not an oversight.
}

-- Where the maintainer supplies init's basics, once the conflict check has
-- passed. Reachable only while status is `init_requested`: a run the conflict
-- check already rejected has moved on to `conflict_found` and offers this
-- nowhere.
surface InitBasics {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: InitializationRun where status = init_requested

    provides:
        ProjectBasicsProvided(
            run, site_title, home_intro?, primary_color?, footer_text?, socials
        )

    @guarantee ConflictsAreCheckedBeforeAnyPrompt
        -- By the time this surface is reachable, every artifact init would
        -- create has already been checked and none was occupied. Nothing is
        -- asked otherwise.
}

-- Why `init` refused to run: every artifact whose path was already occupied.
surface InitConflictReport {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: InitializationRun where status = conflict_found

    exposes:
        for c in run.conflicts:
            c.kind
            c.path

    @guarantee ConflictReportListsEveryOccupiedPath
        -- Every artifact whose path was already occupied is reported at
        -- once, not just the first.
}

-- What `init` created, once it has.
surface InitOutcome {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: InitializationRun where status = scaffolded

    exposes:
        run.site_title
        run.home_intro
        run.primary_color
        run.footer_text

        for h in run.given_socials:
            h.provider
            h.handle

        run.repository_initialized
        run.gitignore_created
}

-- Authoring content into an existing project ---------------------------

-- The `page new` subcommand.
surface PageCreationRequest {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        PageCreationRequested(source, image_source?, path, image?)
}

-- Why `page new` refused to run: every reason the request was blocked.
surface PageCreationBlockedReport {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: PageCreationRun where status = page_creation_blocked

    exposes:
        for b in run.blocks:
            b.reason
            b.detail
}

-- Where the maintainer supplies the rest of a new page's details, once the
-- blocker check has passed. Reachable only while status is `requested`: a
-- run the blocker check already rejected has moved on to `blocked` and
-- offers this nowhere.
surface PageCreationDetails {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: PageCreationRun where status = page_creation_requested

    provides:
        PageCreationDetailsProvided(run, title, sub_title?, icon?, summary?, order?, content?)
}

-- What `page new` created, once it has.
surface PageCreatedOutcome {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: PageCreationRun where status = page_created

    exposes:
        run.given_path
        run.title
        run.sub_title
        run.icon
        run.summary
        run.order
        run.content
        run.content_was_defaulted
        run.given_image_reference
}

-- The `image add` subcommand.
surface ImageRegistrationRequest {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        ImageRegistrationRequested(image_source, dir, file_name)
}

surface ImageRegistrationBlockedReport {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: ImageRegistrationRun where status = image_registration_blocked

    exposes:
        for b in run.blocks:
            b.reason
            b.detail
}

surface ImageRegistrationDetails {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: ImageRegistrationRun where status = image_registration_requested

    provides:
        ImageRegistrationDetailsProvided(run, title?, alt_text)
}

-- What `image add` registered, once it has -- including the reference key a
-- page or notification would use to point at it.
surface ImageRegisteredOutcome {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: ImageRegistrationRun where status = image_registered

    exposes:
        run.reference_key
        run.title
        run.alt_text
}

-- The `page add-image` subcommand.
surface PageImageAttachmentRequest {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        PageImageAttachmentRequested(
            source, image_source, path, dir?, file_name?, existing_reference?, as?
        )
}

surface PageImageAttachmentBlockedReport {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: PageImageAttachmentRun where status = image_attachment_blocked

    exposes:
        for b in run.blocks:
            b.reason
            b.detail
}

-- Exposes effective_role so the maintainer knows, before answering, whether
-- this attachment is about to fill the page's hero slot or join its
-- notifications.
surface PageImageAttachmentDetails {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: PageImageAttachmentRun where status = image_attachment_requested

    exposes:
        run.effective_role

    provides:
        PageImageAttachmentDetailsProvided(run, alt_text?, side?, caption?)
}

surface PageImageAttachedOutcome {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: PageImageAttachmentRun where status = image_attached

    exposes:
        run.given_path
        run.reference_key
        run.effective_role
        run.given_side
        run.effective_caption
}

-- The `social add` subcommand.
surface SocialLinkAdditionRequest {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        SocialLinkAdditionRequested(source, handle?, url?, icon?, brand_color?)
}

-- The `social update` subcommand.
surface SocialLinkUpdateRequest {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        SocialLinkUpdateRequested(source, match, index?, handle?, url?, icon?, brand_color?)
}

-- The `social remove` subcommand.
surface SocialLinkRemovalRequest {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        SocialLinkRemovalRequested(source, match, index?)
}

-- Why a social-link change was rejected, whichever of the three subcommands
-- requested it.
surface SocialLinkChangeRejectedReport {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: SocialLinkChangeRun where status = social_change_rejected

    exposes:
        run.change_kind
        run.rejection
}

-- What a social-link change did, once applied.
surface SocialLinkChangedOutcome {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: SocialLinkChangeRun where status = social_change_applied

    exposes:
        run.change_kind
        run.resulting_url
        run.resulting_provider
}

-- The `footer set` subcommand.
surface FooterUpdateRequest {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        FooterUpdateRequested(source, footer_content)
}

surface FooterUpdatedOutcome {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: FooterUpdateRun where status = footer_updated

    exposes:
        run.footer_content.reference
        run.footer_content.kind
}

-- The `menu add-item` subcommand.
surface MenuLinkAdditionRequest {
    facing _: Maintainer

    provides:
        MenuLinkAdditionRequested(source, url, title?, icon?)
}

surface MenuLinkAdditionRejectedReport {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: MenuLinkAdditionRun where status = menu_link_addition_rejected

    exposes:
        run.rejection
}

-- would_resolve tells the maintainer, at the moment the entry is added,
-- whether it will actually show up in the published menu -- distinct from a
-- rejection, since an internal link naming a path with no page yet is
-- allowed; it just will not resolve until that page exists.
surface MenuLinkAddedOutcome {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: MenuLinkAdditionRun where status = menu_link_added

    exposes:
        run.given_url
        run.given_title
        run.given_icon
        run.kind
        run.would_resolve
}

-- What the maintainer authors in the source tree: main.json and menu.json at
-- the root, a page.json per content directory, and images.json per image
-- directory.
surface SourceTree {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context run: GenerationRun where status in {requested, configured}

    exposes:
        run.mode
        run.source_location
        run.image_source_location
        run.destination
        run.status

    provides:
        SiteConfigurationDeclared(
            run, site_title, footer_content,
            extra_css_files, extra_js_files,
            include_default_css?, include_default_js?,
            background_class?, navbar_color?
        ) when run.status = requested

        MainMenuLinkDeclared(run, position, url, title?, icon?)
            when run.status = configured
        SocialLinkDeclared(run, position, url, icon?, brand_color?)
            when run.status = configured
        PageDeclared(
            run, path, title?, sub_title?, image?, icon?, summary?, content?, order?
        ) when run.status = configured
        ImageDeclared(run, reference_key, source_file, alt_text, title?)
            when run.status = configured
        SourceScanCompleted(run) when run.status = configured

        -- A file that could not be read as what it claims to be. A check run
        -- takes this as a finding and keeps reading; a generate run cannot get
        -- past it and stops.
        SourceProblemDetected(run, kind, location, detail?)
            when run.mode = check

    @guarantee PageJsonIsOptional
        -- A content directory with no page.json still becomes a page: it is
        -- titled after the directory and its content is a bare heading.

    @guarantee DiscoveryIsTheSameInBothModes
        -- A check run walks exactly the trees a generate run would and declares
        -- exactly the same things from them. Anything a check reports on is
        -- something a generate run would have met in the same place.
}

surface PageNotifications {
    facing _: Maintainer

    context page: Page where status = discovered

    exposes:
        page.path
        page.title

    provides:
        PageNotificationDeclared(
            page, side, position, content, title?, image?, url?, color?
        )

    @guarantee NotificationsCoverTheSubtree
        -- Notifications declared here apply to this page and to every page
        -- beneath it that declares none of its own, so a whole section can be
        -- annotated from one place.
}

-- What a visitor sees on a published page. A published site is read-only:
-- there is nothing for a visitor to do.
surface PublishedPage {
    facing _: Visitor

    context page: Page where status = published

    let site = page.run.site_config

    exposes:
        site.site_title
        site.navbar
        site.background
        site.css_links
        site.js_links
        site.footer_content.html

        page.title
        page.sub_title
        page.summary
        page.content.html

        page.main_image.title when page.main_image.status = rendered
        page.main_image.alt_text when page.main_image.status = rendered
        page.main_image.ratio when page.main_image.status = rendered
        page.main_image.variant_widths when page.main_image.status = rendered
        page.main_image.fallback_width when page.main_image.status = rendered
        page.main_image_placement when page.main_image.status = rendered

        for link in page.run.published_main_menu_links:
            link.url
            link.kind
            link.title
            link.icon
            link.dropdown

        for social in page.run.social_links:
            social.url
            social.icon
            social.brand_color

        page.breadcrumb_trail when page.shows_breadcrumbs
        page.side_menu_root when page.shows_side_menu

        for n in page.effective_left_notifications:
            n.kind
            n.title
            n.content.html
            n.url
            n.color
            n.image
            n.image_placement

        for n in page.effective_right_notifications:
            n.kind
            n.title
            n.content.html
            n.url
            n.color
            n.image
            n.image_placement

        for neighbour in page.side_notification_pages:
            neighbour.path
            neighbour.title
            neighbour.sub_title
            neighbour.main_image
        page.side_notification_image_placement
        page.side_notification_color

    @guarantee ImagePlacementDrivesLoading
        -- A page's hero image is placed as `main` and fetched eagerly; images
        -- inside notifications are placed as `sub` and images inside side
        -- notifications as `side`, and both are deferred. Placement also
        -- determines the display size.

    @guarantee SideNotificationsAreNotConfigurable
        -- Side notifications are always styled with the side notification
        -- colour and always advertise neighbouring pages: a page's own
        -- children when it has any, falling back to its siblings only when it
        -- is childless. That choice is fixed policy, not a default the
        -- maintainer can override: unlike left and right notifications, side
        -- notifications carry no configuration at all.

    @guarantee MainMenuOnlyShowsResolvableEntries
        -- Every main menu entry a visitor sees can be followed. An internal
        -- entry naming a path with no page is dropped from the menu and
        -- reported to the maintainer; the visitor never sees a menu item with
        -- no title or a link to nowhere.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- PagePathIsUnique and SingleRootPage are stated invariants with no
-- corresponding check finding, on the reasoning that a directory tree cannot
-- produce two pages at one path or two roots.
open question "If pages ever come from anywhere but directories, does check need findings for PagePathIsUnique and SingleRootPage?"

-- init's menu.json starts with no main-menu entries; the maintainer adds
-- those by hand afterward. Social links are no longer part of this question
-- -- the wizard gathers those now -- but main-menu entries still are not.
open question "Should a later version of init let the wizard add initial main-menu entries, rather than starting empty?"

-- `menu add-item` is deliberately add-only, the same way PageCreationRun and
-- ImageRegistrationRun have no delete/rename counterpart: an entry, once
-- added, can only be changed or removed by hand-editing menu.json.
open question "Should a later version add `menu update-item`/`menu remove-item` alongside `menu add-item`?"
